EC on QEMU

CONTENTS OF THIS FILE
---------------------

 * Introduction
 * Requirements
 * Virtual Hardware
 * Building
 * Testing

INTRODUCTION
------------

Using QEMU to run Embedded Controller (EC) code greatly simplifies and lowers
the learning curve of noobies because no hardware and device programmers are
needed. This method of running EC code is also useful to the experienced
developer by enabling them to write code for virtual hardware and to test the
code under different memory configurations. For example, code could be
developed for a new EC before the EC was designed into a device.

REQUIREMENTS
------------

The following are required:

 * Git

 * EC codebase (https://www.chromium.org/chromium-os/ec-development)

VIRTUAL HARDWARE
----------------

The "QEMU" window is the user interface to the virtual hardware. It has
a chromeos lightbar, LCD, 8-LEDs, temperature sensor, and 4-button arrow
key pad.

 * Lightbar    - typical chromeos lightbar connected to I2C
                 There are four led segments located at the top of the window.

 * LCD         - 132 x 64 Dot Matrix display connected to I2C
		 The display is located just under the lightbar.
		 Controller for the LCD is SSD0303

 * LEDs        - 8 LEDs connected to GPIOs
		 The LEDs are located at the bottom of the window.

 * Temp Sensor - Temp Sensor connected to an ADC that returns an increasing
		 value

 * Key Pad     - 4-button arrow key pad connected to GPIO
		 These buttons are mapped to the arrow keys on the keyboard.

BUILDING
--------

Build QEMU
 * download qemu-2.9.0.tar.xy from www.qemu.org

 * tar xf qemu-2.9.0.tar.xy

 * cd qemu-2.9.0

 * git apply <path_to_ec>/ec/board/qemu_demo/qemu_2.9.0.patch

 * ./configure --target-list=arm-softmmu

 * make -j

Build qemu_demo
 * cd <path_to_ec>/ec

 * make -j BOARD=qemu_demo

TESTING
-------

 * go to the qemu_2.9.0 directory

 * ./arm-softmmu/qemu-system-arm -M qemu_demo \
      -kernel <path_to_ec>/ec/build/qemu_demo/ec.bin -serial stdio -show-cursor

 * A window titled "QEMU" should open with "EC ON QEMU" displayed and the
   lightbar flashing multiple colors. The console should display something
   similar to the following:

      --- UART initialized after reboot ---
      [Reset cause: unknown]
      [Image: RW, qemu_demo_v1.1.6600-deb50d961+ 2017-07-07 11:18:02]
      [0.000000 Inits done]
      [0.000000 LB task starting]
      [0.000000 LB state initialized]
      [0.000000 LB running cur_seq 1 S5. prev_seq 1 S5]
      [0.000000 LB_off]
      Console is enabled; type HELP for help.
      > [0.000000 LB task starting]
      [0.000000 LB state initialized]
      [0.000000 LB running cur_seq 1 S5. prev_seq 1 S5]
      [0.000000 LB_off]


   To use the arrow keys, give the QEMU window focus by clicking the
   mouse inside the window. To exit prest CTRL-ALT

     Pressing the up-arrow key should toggle led0 and led1.
     Pressing the right-arrow key should toggle led2 and led3.
     Pressing the down-arrow key should toggle led4 and led5.
     Pressing the left-arrow key should toggle led6 and led7.


   At the console, type the following:
      > lcdprint 0 This is cool
      > lcdprint 1 Line 2 :-)

   To read the increasing value from the Temp Sensor, type the following:
      > adc
